        <div id="uef" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Efficiency Ratings Actually Mean
          </h2>

          <p>
            Water heater efficiency is reported as Uniform Energy Factor, or UEF. It reflects how much of the gas energy ends up as hot water at the tap over a standard day of use, including standby losses as the tank sits. Standard atmospheric gas tanks typically sit in the low 0.6s, higher-efficiency non-condensing models in the high 0.6s to 0.7s, and condensing models above 0.8.
          </p>

          <p>
            UEF is rated within draw patterns, labeled very small, low, medium, or high, based on first-hour rating. Comparing a medium-draw model's UEF with a high-draw model's is less meaningful than comparing within the same draw pattern. The EnergyGuide label also gives an estimated yearly energy cost based on national average prices, which is a convenient way to compare two specific models.
          </p>

          <p>
            The real-world savings from higher efficiency depend on how much hot water you use and what you pay for gas. A family of four using a lot of hot water benefits much more from a condensing heater than a single person. Use the yearly cost estimate on each label, adjust for your local gas price, and divide the difference into the extra installed cost to estimate payback.
          </p>

          <p>
            Energy Star certified gas tanks must meet a higher efficiency threshold than federal minimums. Certification can unlock utility rebates in some areas, so check with your gas company before buying. Rebate programs change often, and some require pre-approval or installation by a participating contractor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compare" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Gas Tank Versus Tankless, Electric, and Heat Pump
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gas tanks offer the best combination of low purchase price, fast recovery, and simple installation where gas and venting already exist. Their main drawbacks are standby heat loss, a lifespan of roughly 8 to 12 years, and the combustion safety considerations that come with any gas appliance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas tankless heaters deliver continuous hot water and last longer, often 15 to 20 years, but cost much more to install, usually need a larger gas line and new venting, and can struggle to supply several fixtures at once in cold-water climates. They make sense for long ownership horizons and households that value endless hot water over upfront cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electric resistance tanks are cheap to buy and simple to install, but they recover slowly and typically cost more to run than gas where gas prices are moderate. Heat pump water heaters are the most efficient electric option, using a fraction of the electricity of a resistance tank. If you are weighing a switch away from gas, our guide to the <a href="/best-heat-pump-water-heater/">best heat pump water heaters</a> covers space, noise, and payback.
          </p>

