          <p>
            For a like-for-like replacement where gas and a working vent already exist, a gas tank remains the simplest, cheapest choice. The more you would need to change, such as venting, gas line, or electrical work, the more the alternatives deserve a look.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Installation, Code, and Safety Basics
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gas water heater installation involves gas piping, combustion venting, and pressure-relief safety devices, and most jurisdictions require a permit. Many homeowners can physically do the work, but the consequences of a venting or gas mistake are serious. Hiring a licensed plumber is the safer path for most people, and some manufacturers tie warranty coverage to proper installation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every tank needs a temperature and pressure relief valve with a discharge pipe running to within a few inches of the floor or to an approved drain. Many codes also require a thermal expansion tank when the home has a check valve or pressure-reducing valve on the main water line. In earthquake regions, including all of California, codes require seismic straps on water heaters.
          </p>

          <p>
            Venting must be sized correctly, slope upward, and connect securely. A backdrafting atmospheric heater can spill carbon monoxide into the home. After installation, check for proper draft by holding a match or incense stick near the draft hood while the burner runs; smoke should be drawn into the vent. Install carbon monoxide alarms near sleeping areas and on each level of the home.
          </p>

          <p>
            Combustion air matters, especially in small utility rooms. A heater in a tight closet may need louvered doors or ducted outdoor air. Be careful with large exhaust fans in a tight house, including bath fans, kitchen hoods, and a <a href="/best-whole-house-fan/">whole house fan</a>, all of which can depressurize the house and pull exhaust back down an atmospheric vent if makeup air is lacking.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SAFETY</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If you smell gas, leave first</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Do not operate switches, phones, or appliances near the smell. Get everyone out, then call the gas utility or 911 from outside. Never try to relight a pilot or troubleshoot a heater while you can smell gas.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Maintenance That Adds Years
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sediment is the most common enemy. Minerals in the water settle on the tank bottom, where they insulate the water from the burner, cause rumbling or popping noises as trapped water boils, and overheat the steel. Draining a few gallons from the drain valve every year, or flushing more thoroughly in hard-water areas, slows that buildup.
          </p>

          <p>
            The anode rod is what protects the steel tank from corrosion. It is a sacrificial metal rod that slowly corrodes instead of the tank. Once it is depleted, the tank itself starts to corrode and eventually leaks. Checking the anode every three to five years, and replacing it when it is down to the core wire, can meaningfully extend the life of a tank. It requires a large socket and sometimes a flexible rod for low ceilings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test the temperature and pressure relief valve annually by lifting the lever briefly, standing clear of the discharge pipe. If it does not release water, or keeps dripping afterward, have it replaced. Set the thermostat to about 120 degrees, which reduces scald risk and standby loss while still being hot enough for most households.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the area around the heater clear. Stored boxes, paint cans, gasoline, and solvents near a gas burner are a fire hazard, and clutter can block combustion air. Check the vent connector for rust or gaps each year, and look at the burner flame; it should be mostly blue. A lazy yellow flame suggests a combustion problem worth a service call.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What It Costs to Buy, Install, and Run
          </h2>

          <p>
            At the time of writing, standard 40- and 50-gallon atmospheric gas tanks cost roughly $600 to $1,100 for the unit, power-vent models roughly $1,500 to $2,300, and condensing models roughly $2,500 to $3,500. Professional installation for a like-for-like replacement often adds $800 to $1,500, more if venting, gas piping, or code upgrades are needed.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Installed Ranges</p>
