          </div>

          <p>
            Running costs depend on usage and gas price. The EnergyGuide label on each heater estimates yearly operating cost at national average prices, typically a few hundred dollars a year for a standard 50-gallon gas tank serving a family. A higher-efficiency model lowers that figure, but the savings often take years to cover the higher installed cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Budget for extras on replacement day: a new expansion tank, earthquake straps, a drain pan with a drain line if the heater is above finished space, new flexible water connectors, and haul-away of the old unit. Many installers include some of these; ask for a line-item quote so you can compare bids fairly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Hot Water During a Power Outage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Standard atmospheric gas water heaters with a standing pilot and a thermocouple-powered gas valve need no electricity, so they keep producing hot water during a power outage. That is one of the main reasons many people choose them, especially in areas with frequent storms.
          </p>

          <p>
            Power-vent, condensing, and many newer electronic-ignition models need electricity for the blower, controls, or igniter, so they stop heating when the power goes out. The water already in the tank stays hot for a while, but it will not be reheated. If outages are common where you live and hot water matters during them, check the specification carefully before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some households run power-vent heaters from a generator or a battery backup during outages. The blower and controls draw relatively little power, but the heater must be connected safely through a transfer switch or approved inlet, never by backfeeding a household outlet. Our guide to the <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generators for power outages</a> covers how to do that correctly.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you own a heater that needs power and want hot water in outages, the simplest option at replacement time is a standard atmospheric model, provided the venting supports it. If the venting requires a power-vent or condensing unit, a properly installed backup power source is the alternative.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="propane" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Natural Gas or Propane
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most gas water heaters are sold in separate natural gas and liquid propane versions, and the two are not interchangeable out of the box. Propane burns at a higher supply pressure and needs a different orifice and gas valve setting. Some manufacturers sell conversion kits for specific models, but many do not, and a conversion must be done by a qualified technician following the manufacturer instructions. The simplest approach is to buy the version that matches your fuel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the rating plate on your current heater and the fuel your other appliances use before ordering. Buying the wrong version is a common online-order mistake, and returning a 150-pound water heater is an unpleasant afternoon. Home center listings usually show NG or LP in the model number or title.
          </p>

          <p>
            Propane costs more per unit of heat than natural gas in most of the country, which shifts the efficiency math. For a propane household with high hot-water use, the savings from a condensing model or a heat pump water heater arrive faster than they would on cheap natural gas. It is worth running the numbers on both before replacing a propane tank like for like.
          </p>

          <p>
            Propane tank capacity also matters. A water heater is one of the larger propane loads in a home, and adding a high-input heater can mean more frequent deliveries. If the household relies on propane for heating as well, ask your supplier whether the tank and regulator are sized for the combined load, especially in cold weather.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Repair or Replace?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Some failures are cheap repairs. A thermocouple, thermostat, gas valve, or igniter can be replaced at a fraction of the cost of a new heater, and these parts commonly fail on otherwise healthy tanks. A professional can diagnose most of these quickly. If the heater is less than about seven years old and the tank is sound, repair is usually worth it.
          </p>

          <p>
            A leaking tank cannot be repaired. Water pooling under the heater from the tank itself, rather than from a fitting or the relief valve discharge, means the steel has corroded through. Replace it promptly, because a small leak can become a large one. Check fittings and the relief valve first, as those leaks are fixable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Age is the other factor. Many gas tanks last 8 to 12 years, with hard water and skipped maintenance shortening that. If the heater is past 10 years and showing signs of trouble, such as rusty hot water, loud rumbling, or a slow leak, replacement is generally the better investment than another repair.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan ahead if possible. Replacing a heater on your schedule, rather than after it fails and floods the basement, gives you time to compare quotes, consider higher-efficiency options, and check rebates. If the heater sits above finished space, a drain pan and an automatic shutoff valve are inexpensive insurance. And if the old heater was in a damp basement, a dehumidifier from our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier guide</a> helps protect the new one from external corrosion.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing by tank gallons alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two 50-gallon tanks may supply very different volumes during your busiest hour, because burner size changes recovery. Compare first-hour ratings on the EnergyGuide label against your peak-hour use instead. Buying on gallons alone is how families end up with a tank that runs cold every morning.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the old vent still works</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If the furnace was upgraded to a high-efficiency model, the chimney it once shared may now be too large for a water heater alone, which can cause poor draft and condensation. Have the installer confirm the vent is suitable, or plan for a power-vent or direct-vent model.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the expansion tank</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Homes with a check valve or pressure-reducing valve on the main line trap expanding hot water, raising pressure and stressing the tank and relief valve. Many codes require a thermal expansion tank in that situation. Leaving it out can shorten the heater's life and cause relief valve discharges.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never touching the anode or drain valve</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sediment and a spent anode rod are the main reasons gas tanks fail early. Draining some water every year and checking the anode every few years are simple, inexpensive tasks that can add years of life. Ignoring both until the tank leaks is the most expensive maintenance plan.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing flammables near the heater</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gasoline, paint thinner, and similar products give off vapors that can be ignited by a gas burner or pilot. Boxes and clutter also block the combustion air the heater needs. Keep the area around the heater clear and store flammables in a separate space, ideally outside the house.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How big a gas water heater does a family of four need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most families of four do well with a 50-gallon gas tank with a first-hour rating in the 75 to 90 gallon range. If several people shower back to back, or there is a large soaking tub, look for a higher first-hour rating or a 75-gallon unit. Check the EnergyGuide label for the specific model's figure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How is first-hour rating different from tank size?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tank size is how much water the heater stores. First-hour rating is how much hot water it can supply in one hour starting with a full tank, including what the burner reheats during that hour. Because burner sizes vary, two tanks of the same size can have quite different first-hour ratings, which is why it is the better sizing number.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the typical lifespan of a gas tank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many gas tank water heaters last roughly 8 to 12 years. Hard water, high usage, and skipped maintenance shorten that range, while annual draining and replacing the anode rod when it is depleted can extend it. Warranties of six to twelve years give a rough sense of how long the maker expects the tank to hold up.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will I have hot water when the power goes out?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Standard atmospheric models with a standing pilot and a thermocouple-powered gas valve keep heating water without electricity. Power-vent, condensing, and many electronic-ignition models need power for their blower or controls and stop heating during an outage, though the tank stays warm for a while. Check the specifications before buying if outages are common.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What causes rumbling or popping in a gas water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually sediment. Minerals settle on the tank bottom, trap small pockets of water that boil against the hot steel, and make rumbling or popping sounds. Draining and flushing the tank can reduce it. Persistent loud noise in an older heater often signals heavy buildup, reduced efficiency, and a tank that may be nearing the end of its life.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a power-vent water heater worth the extra cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is worth it when you need it, typically when there is no suitable chimney, when the furnace was upgraded and the shared chimney is no longer appropriate, or when the heater must be relocated. Otherwise, a standard atmospheric model is cheaper to buy and works without electricity. Power-vent models also add a blower that hums when running.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a gas water heater myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is possible, but most jurisdictions require a permit, and the work involves gas piping, venting, and relief valve installation where mistakes can cause carbon monoxide exposure, gas leaks, or tank failure. Many homeowners hire a licensed plumber, and some manufacturers require professional installation for full warranty coverage. If you do it yourself, follow the manual and get the inspection.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
